      "knowledgeBaseRetrievedContents": [
        "{Title: Heating and cooling basics}\n\n{H1: Heating and cooling basics, H2: Maintenance and tune-ups}\n## Maintenance and tune-ups\n\nA tune-up is a yearly check and cleaning of equipment that still works. For heating it's best done in the fall, before the cold; for cooling, in the spring. A technician typically inspects and cleans burners, checks safety controls and the flue on gas equipment, tests the thermostat, checks electrical connections, and on air conditioners cleans the coils and checks refrigerant. Regular tune-ups help equipment last longer and catch small problems early.",
        "{Title: Heating and cooling basics}\n\n{H1: Heating and cooling basics}\n## Equipment age\n\nMany boilers last 15 to 30 years, furnaces 15 to 20, and central air conditioners and heat pumps 10 to 15, depending on care and use.\n## Carbon monoxide\n\nCarbon monoxide is a gas with no color or smell, made when fuel burns. Equipment that burns gas or oil, like boilers, furnaces and water heaters, needs working vents. New York City requires carbon monoxide alarms in homes."
      ],
